# Cursor AI Rules for Open Spaces Comocamp

## Project Overview
This is an event-sourced Node.js application for managing open spaces conferences. The draw io diagram contains the event model that matches the slices

Don't change the bootstap code if working on slice logic.

A slice has these components and rules:
 - name: string (required)
 - navigation: navigation_node (required)
 - initial_state: any (required)
 - event_handlers: object (required)
 - refinement_function: function (required)
 - exceptions: object (optional)
 - test_timelines: array of timeline_node (optional)
 - processor: processor_node (optional)

Purpose: Slices are the core architectural units that encapsulate business logic, handle HTTP routing, define how one step in a workflow changes state or provides a certian view of state

A navigation_node has these properties and rules:
 - direction: "input" | "output" (required)
 - path: string (required)
 - web_data: function (optional)
 - access_checks: array of functions (optional)
 - rules:
    - if direction is "input": next_path is required, view is not allowed
    - if direction is "output": view is required, next_path is not allowed
 - next_path: string | function (required when direction is "input")
 - view: string (required when direction is "output")

Purpose: Navigation nodes define HTTP routing configuration, determining whether a slice handles user input (POST requests) or displays output (GET requests), and specifying the URL paths, access controls, and data extraction from HTTP requests.

A processor_node has these properties and rules:
 - execution: "immediate" | "timed" (required)
 - todo_list_slice: name (required)
 - triggering_events: array of events (required)
 - processor_filter: function (required)
 - processor_action: function (required)

Purpose: Processor nodes enable background processing by automatically responding to specific events, filtering todo items from a designated slice, and executing actions like generating conference IDs or other automated tasks.

A timeline_node has these properties and rules:
 - timeline_name: string (required)
 - checkpoints: array of checkpoint_node (required)

Purpose: Timeline nodes define test scenarios that validate slice behavior by replaying sequences of events and verifying expected outcomes on, enabling comprehensive testing of business logic flows.

A checkpoint_node has these properties and rules:
 - event: object (optional)
 - check: string (optional)
 - parameter: any (optional)
 - exception: string (optional)
 - rules:
    - either event or (check with parameter or exception) must be present
    - if check is present, either parameter or exception must be present

Purpose: Checkpoint nodes represent individual test steps that either specify events on the timeline or verify that the slice produces expected results (events, state views or exceptions) when given specific parameters.